-- consensus.belief_logit(rating, rd) -- eff_mu expressed as log-odds. THE weight for any
-- ranked surface, and the only one.
--
-- eff_mu (rating - 2*rd) is a conservative BOUND in rating points, not a log-odds.
-- Exponentiating it directly ranks the same and normalises to nothing meaningful, because
-- the units are wrong: exp() of a rating point needs a divisor, and every divisor invented
-- for it is a dial. This converts the bound into Glicko-2's own scale instead, so exp() of
-- the result is a Bradley-Terry weight on belief.
--
-- NO DIAL IS INTRODUCED. 1500 comes from consensus.glicko2_neutral_mu(), the body the folder
-- already uses, and 400/ln(10) is Glicko-2's definitional rating scale -- the constant that
-- makes its underlying logistic a base-10 400-point curve. Neither is free to choose.
--
-- WHY NOT g(phi)*mu. Glicko-2's own attenuation applied to the RAW rating,
-- g(phi) = 1/sqrt(1 + 3*phi^2/pi^2) with phi = rd/(400/ln 10), was written here first and
-- then DELETED rather than kept beside this one. It penalises uncertainty by a different
-- law: eff_mu SUBTRACTS 2*rd without bound, g(phi) MULTIPLIES by a saturating factor in
-- (0,1]. Measured on one pair -- rating 2000/rd 100 gives eff_mu 1800 and g(phi)*mu 2.7434,
-- rating 2100/rd 200 gives eff_mu 1700 and 2.9159 -- the two order the same two edges
-- oppositely. A surface that ORDERS by one and WEIGHTS by the other has a top row that is
-- not its heaviest row, so "take the best" and "sample the distribution" return different
-- objects from one call. INVENTION §5 settles which survives: belief is rating - 2*rd and
-- all ranked reads order by it, so the weight moved onto belief rather than the order
-- moving off it.
--
-- It was deleted rather than left uncalled for two reasons. A correct-looking second weight
-- standing beside the real one is the trap cmake/toolchains/gcc-deterministic.cmake already
-- is in this tree -- authoritative name, defensible body, silently different answer. And an
-- uncalled function has no losing condition: nothing exercises it, so nothing can ever show
-- it wrong.
--
-- NOT a replacement for eff_mu. eff_mu stays the indexed ranking key (#860, the
-- eff-mu-inlining-law) and this is never an ORDER BY; it is strictly monotone in eff_mu, so
-- ordering by either gives the same sequence, and the indexes only match the literal form.
CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION consensus.belief_logit(p_rating bigint, p_rd bigint)
    RETURNS double precision
    LANGUAGE sql LAPLACE_IMMUTABLE_STRICT
BEGIN ATOMIC
    SELECT ((consensus.eff_mu(p_rating, p_rd) - consensus.glicko2_neutral_mu()) / 1e9)
         / (400 / ln(10));
END;
